.TH TINYGL 2
.SH NAME
tglinit, tglresize, tglread, tglclose \- classic OpenGL subset over GPU files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <u.h>
.br
.B #include <libc.h>
.br
.B #include <GL/gl.h>
.br
.B #include <tinygl.h>
.PP
.B
void tglinit(char *device, int width, int height)
.PP
.B
void tglresize(int width, int height)
.PP
.B
void tglread(void *pixels, int bytes)
.PP
.B
void tglclose(void)
.SH DESCRIPTION
TinyGL implements a subset of classic OpenGL using native Plan 9 C.
Vertex positions are transformed and projected on the GPU.
Vertex lighting, texture-coordinate generation and the eye coordinates they
need remain on the CPU. Clipping, interpolation, rasterization and depth
testing use VirGL through
.IR gpu (3).
Six additional user clip planes are evaluated on the CPU.
.PP
.I Tglinit
creates the process's single current context and an off-screen color/depth
target of the requested size.
.I Device
is the directory containing the GPU's
.B clone
and
.B caps
files; nil selects
.BR #∇ .
The directory can be bound elsewhere in the caller's namespace.
Both dimensions must be between 1 and 2048.
The initial viewport covers the target.
.PP
The supported GL operations include immediate points, lines, triangles,
strips, fans, quads and convex polygons; indexed client arrays and buffer
objects; flat and smooth colors; matrix stacks and projections;
face culling; one- and two-sided vertex lighting and materials;
display lists; alpha testing and blending; depth functions and write masks;
polygon offset; color write masks and scissor rectangles; and clearing color
and depth. Clears honor the scissor and write masks. Lines and points support
sizes from 1 through 64 pixels. Points also support distance attenuation and
texture sprites. Polygons may be filled, outlined or drawn as points.
The header declares the available entry points, not a complete GL API.
.PP
.B glClipPlane
captures a plane equation in eye coordinates using the inverse transpose
of the current modelview matrix. Enable planes individually with
.B GL_CLIP_PLANE0
through
.BR GL_CLIP_PLANE5 .
Points, lines and polygons retain the nonnegative half-space.
.B glGetClipPlane
returns the captured equation. Display lists transform equations when
executed, not when compiled.
.PP
Four texture units provide two-dimensional textures up to 2048 by 2048,
nearest and linear filtering, mipmaps, repeat/clamp wrapping, texture
matrices and object-linear, eye-linear and sphere-map coordinate generation.
Images accept unsigned-byte RGB, RGBA, alpha, luminance and
luminance-alpha pixels, with configurable unpack alignment, row length
and skipped rows/pixels. Packed RGB565, RGBA5551 and RGBA4444 unsigned-short
pixels are also supported. Texture borders must be zero.
Mipmaps may be supplied as individual levels or generated on image replacement
with
.BR GL_GENERATE_MIPMAP_SGIS .
Subimages replace rectangular regions without redefining the texture.
Texture and framebuffer readback use the corresponding pack parameters;
framebuffer coordinates start at the lower left and must lie inside the target.
Fixed-function texture environments include modulate, replace, decal, add,
blend and combine (replace, modulate, add, signed add and interpolate).
.PP
Array strides are byte counts; zero means tightly packed.
Texture images are retained in guest memory. Under resource pressure,
unused GPU copies are evicted in order of least recent use and uploaded
again when needed. Currently enabled textures are kept resident during a draw.
.PP
Buffer objects store array and index data in guest memory for array capture,
lighting and user clipping.
Display lists own the array values and texture images obtained during
compilation, independently of subsequent buffer or image changes.
List names are in the range 1 through 1023 and nesting is limited to 64.
Generated names are reserved but remain undefined until
.BR glEndList .
Redefinition preserves the old list until compilation finishes.
Calls to undefined lists do nothing.
.PP
.I Tglresize
finishes pending drawing and replaces the render target, releasing the old
resources. Its contents become undefined.
It does not change GL state or the viewport: call
.B glViewport
as appropriate for the new size.
Viewport dimensions may be zero and are limited to 4096; coordinates are
limited to the range -4096 through 4096.
.PP
.I Tglread
finishes pending drawing, downloads the color target and copies it into
.IR pixels .
.I Bytes
must be at least width times height times four.
Pixels are tightly packed, top row first, in the XRGB32 byte layout used by
.IR draw (2).
GL window coordinates still have their origin at the lower left.
The application owns the buffer and chooses how to display or save it.
.PP
.B glFlush
submits pending primitives and waits for completion.
State changes end the current vertex batch without necessarily submitting it.
Full command buffers submit automatically; vertex storage is reused only
after pending work completes.
.I Tglclose
finishes pending drawing, closes the GPU context and frees its allocations.
A subsequent
.I tglinit
creates fresh state.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
uchar pixels[64*64*4];

tglinit(nil, 64, 64);
glClearColor(0, 0, 0, 1);
glClear(GL_COLOR_BUFFER_BIT | GL_DEPTH_BUFFER_BIT);
glBegin(GL_TRIANGLES);
glColor3f(1, 0, 0); glVertex2f(-.8, -.8);
glColor3f(0, 1, 0); glVertex2f(.8, -.8);
glColor3f(0, 0, 1); glVertex2f(0, .8);
glEnd();
tglread(pixels, sizeof pixels);
tglclose();
.EE
